# The system services: what init starts at every boot, before the user list,
# /data/services.txt. The build puts this file in both images as
# /etc/claudeos/services, and the kernel checks it at boot against
# /etc/claudeos/checksums, so it is changed here and rebuilt, never on the
# board. The format is described in the README, under "Services".
#
# name  policy  options  command

# The clock. BusyBox ntpd asks the servers /etc/ntp.conf names, which only the
# board image has, so the test images never wait on servers across the
# internet. `-n -q` keeps ntpd in the foreground and makes it exit once the
# clock has been set or found within a second.
#
# busybox is on the card, as /usr/bin/busybox. With /data not mounted it does
# not exist, and ntpd is reported as could not be started and tried again
# after each backoff wait, so a card that is mounted late still gets the clock
# set. It is not a needs= option, which is looked at once, at boot.
#
# every=6h: after a run that exits 0, the next is six hours later. A board's
# crystal keeps to some tens of parts per million, a second or two over six
# hours, so the clock is back around the second ntpd -q does not correct.
#
# backoff=30s-10m: after a failed run the next is 30 s later, doubling with each
# failure in a row to ten minutes. The usual failure at boot is a network not up
# yet, and a WiFi join and a DHCP lease take tens of seconds. The cap means a
# network that comes up late still gives the date within ten minutes of it.
#
# limit=180s: BusyBox 1.36.1's ntpd -q ends itself about ten seconds after it
# starts if no server has answered, and fifty seconds after the first answer,
# but notices that only between name lookups, which take about ten seconds each
# when unanswered, so a run ends on its own within about ninety seconds. Three
# minutes is that with room, and it is short against the ten-minute cap, so no
# run lasts long enough to reset the count of failures.
ntpd  always  needs=/etc/ntp.conf every=6h limit=180s backoff=30s-10m  /usr/bin/busybox ntpd -n -q
